const sixtySummersOnKestrelGlacierDigitalShsatPracticeTest3PassageText = "In 1966, a team of glaciologists hammered a row of steel stakes into the ice of the Kestrel Glacier, a slow river of ice threading down a valley in the northern Cascade Range. Their goal was modest: to measure, year by year, exactly how far the glacier's leading edge — its terminus — advanced or retreated. Nobody on that first team expected the project to still be running six decades later, and nobody expected the record it would eventually produce to become one of the longest continuous glacier measurements in North America. The station itself amounts to little more than a plywood cabin and a shed of surveying equipment, staffed each summer by two or three graduate students who hike the final six miles in on foot because no road reaches that far up the valley.\n\nA glacier's terminus is not a fixed boundary; it is closer to a bank balance, rising when snowfall in the mountains above exceeds summer melting and falling when melting wins out. Through most of the 1960s and 1970s, Kestrel's terminus barely moved, drifting a few meters forward some years and a few meters back the next, the kind of small fluctuation researchers expect from a glacier in rough equilibrium with its climate. Early logbooks from the station record this steadiness almost with relief, one 1974 entry noting only that \"the terminus sits within a stone's throw of last year's marker, as it has for three summers running.\" That pattern held for almost twenty years before it began, gradually and then unmistakably, to break down.\n\nThe measurements in the table below, drawn from the research station's public archive, show the glacier's average annual retreat by decade since monitoring began. A glacier \"retreats\" when its terminus moves back up the valley rather than advancing down it; the table reports how many meters, on average, the terminus moved back each year during that decade. The figures make clear that Kestrel's retreat has not simply continued at a steady pace — it has accelerated, with the most recent decade measuring more than nine times the retreat rate recorded in the 1970s.\n\nResearchers attribute the acceleration to two compounding factors rather than one. Regional summer temperatures near the glacier have risen by just over two degrees Celsius since monitoring began, which on its own would explain a gradual increase in melting. But a glacier's own surface plays a role as well: bare, dust-streaked ice absorbs more sunlight than the fresh, reflective snow that once covered it for most of the year, so as Kestrel's upper reaches spend more summer days exposed rather than snow-covered, they absorb heat more efficiently and melt faster still. Each of the two effects makes the other worse, a relationship climate scientists describe as a feedback loop — one change quietly setting the stage for the next, rather than the two arriving independently. Station researchers first noticed the pattern not in the temperature logs but in old photographs, comparing a 1978 slide of a bright, snow-covered upper glacier against a nearly identical camera angle from 2015 that showed long stretches of dull gray ice exposed well into August.\n\nThe consequences reach well beyond the glacier's own boundaries. Roughly forty thousand residents downstream draw drinking water from the river Kestrel feeds, and for decades that river's summer flow depended on a predictable contribution of glacial meltwater arriving just as mountain snowpack ran low. Hydrologists studying the watershed now warn that this contribution will not simply continue; a retreating glacier still adds meltwater to the river for a period, sometimes increasing flow in the short term as stored ice is released faster than it can be replenished, but a glacier small enough eventually stops contributing meaningfully at all. Kestrel's own ice volume has already fallen by roughly a third since the first stakes were driven into it, and current models suggest the glacier could stop functioning as a meaningful water source within the lifetime of children born downstream today.\n\nWhat keeps researchers returning to the valley each summer is not only Kestrel's local significance but its value as a record. Because the station's methods have barely changed since 1966 — the same type of surveying stake, measured at the same time of year, against the same fixed benchmarks on the valley wall — the archive offers something rarer than a single alarming statistic: an unbroken, directly comparable measurement of exactly how one glacier's response to a warming climate has changed shape over sixty years, not just in degree but in speed. A newer generation of researchers has begun supplementing the stakes with satellite imaging, but the station's director insists the older method stay in place alongside it, arguing that sixty years of consistent, unglamorous fieldwork is exactly what makes the newer, flashier data trustworthy in the first place.";

